income-statement quarterly] is the most concerning near-term risk. This trajectory, if structural rather than cyclical (mix shift toward lower-margin government cost-plus work, or fixed-price contract losses), would substantially impair the FCF trajectory needed to service the 2030 maturity. The revenue growth acceleration (Q3 +28.9% YoY) driven by contract ramp-ups creates apparent earnings momentum, but gross margins have simultaneously compressed from 24-25% to 21%, indicating pricing or cost pressures are not being absorbed — likely fixed-price contract execution risk on new programs. Forward FCF growth estimates of -40.1% for FY2025 [Source: FMP Growth Rates] confirm the market expects near-term FCF deterioration even as revenue grows, which is an unusual and concerning divergence.\n\nOn the downside scenario: if operating margin stabilizes at 9% (vs. the 13-15% prior baseline), annual EBITDA would approximate $450-480M on ~$3.2B revenue. After ~$80M interest expense (implied at market rates on $2B debt), $150M+ CapEx, and taxes, FCF might be $200-250M annually through 2029, leaving a cumulative ~$1B against $2.05B in maturities ($400M+$400M+$1.25B) from 2028-2030. This scenario mandates refinancing and creates material execution risk. The base case requires margin recovery to 12-13% — plausible given naval nuclear demand, but not guaranteed given recent trajectory. Covenant structure is not disclosed in available XBRL data, creating a secondary risk if leverage covenants constrain operational flexibility. The SBC figure of -$39.46B in Q4 [Source: FMP /cash-flow-statement] appears to be a data error and is flagged accordingly.",
